Campus Universe — UI/UX upgrade for Claude Code

Date: 18 September 2026. Apply to the existing application.

1. Outcome

Make Campus Universe feel like a university-focused discovery product: an explorable map, a readable founder network, and a fast company list, all backed by the same evidence. A user should understand what a company builds, why its founder is interesting and what to investigate next within 30 seconds.

Use UNIVERSITY_SOURCES_SHORTLIST.md for sourcing implementation. This file defines the interface upgrade. Preserve existing records, notes, assessments, source provenance, working adapters and routes wherever practical. These instructions are a build specification, not a claim that the application has already been changed.

2. References and limits

YCGlobe: accessible page content includes search, filters, university discovery, founder/company modes, and a database view alongside its globe concept. Borrow the interconnected exploration model. Do not import displayed counts, sample records or marketing claims into Campus Universe.

BuildList: accessible homepage content exposes clear discovery categories, search, filters, saved items and structured records. Borrow concise labels and readable company information.

BuildList Founder Networks: this route could not be inspected during this review. The network design below is a proposed adaptation of the user's requested concept, not a verified description or pixel reproduction of that page.

The supplied Campus Universe screenshots are the baseline for the issues below. Network and Globe implementation were not shown; inspect the actual repository before changing them.

Do not block the upgrade on accessing the references. Implement this explicit design and verify it in the running application.

3. What to change first

Make the product description and reason to investigate visible in every company row and drawer.

Replace the wide, repetitive sidebar and status-heavy table with a compact exploration layout.

Give Penn and Berkeley first-class navigation and honest source-coverage states.

Make Table, Network and Globe interchangeable views with persistent filters and selected entities.

Replace long technical explanations with short labels, useful empty states and next actions.

Enrich the actual records. A polished empty interface does not fulfill the sourcing workflow.

Do not add fake companies, synthetic relationships, random scores, inferred funding totals or fabricated metrics to make the screens look complete.

4. Navigation and page structure

Top bar: Campus Universe · Explore · Shortlist · Signals · Pipeline · Sources. Put Methodology and Settings inside a compact utility menu. Keep current URLs compatible through aliases or redirects where needed.

Explore is the default destination. Its persistent toolbar contains:

Search: “Search founders, companies, projects…”

View switch: Globe | Network | Table.

Entity switch: Companies | Builders. Builders includes people and projects with explicit type labels.

University, sector, eligibility and research-status filters; advanced filters in a popover.

Active-filter chips, result count, clear filters and a modest “Scan sources” action.

First visit on desktop: Globe with the selected Penn and Berkeley campuses visible, plus a compact result tray. Remember the user's last view afterward. On small screens, default to Table. If the globe cannot render, use a usable 2D map or Table with the same selection; explain briefly.

Represent filter/view/entity selection in the URL where practical. Browser Back restores the previous selection. Do not put private notes or private contact information in the URL.

