6. University rail

Show:

All campuses

Penn

Berkeley

More universities…

A university row shows its short name, unique matching company count and coverage indicator. Source-coverage states are Not scanned / Partial / Up to date / Needs attention. Define “Up to date” against that campus's configured sources, never against all startups at the university.

Clicking a university filters every view and focuses its map/network context. University detail exposes programs, builders, companies and source coverage. Counts must be query-derived and typed; do not claim “8 candidates across 5 campuses” when four campuses have no collection coverage.

Below universities, show compact saved views such as All discovered, Ready to review and Needs research. Use one consistent mapping to existing queues. Source implementation logs belong on Sources, not in the main navigation rail.

7. Globe view

Provide two explicit geographic modes:

University geography (default): markers represent campuses and their associated records.

Company HQ: markers represent independently sourced headquarters locations.

Never place a company at Penn or Berkeley and imply that the university is its HQ. Do not locate people at private residences. Unknown HQ stays in the result list under “Location unknown.”

Interactions:

Click Penn or Berkeley to open a campus summary with company, builder and source counts plus preview results.

Click a preview company to open its drawer; the selected campus remains in view.

Hover reveals a short campus label and typed counts, not a paragraph.

Cluster nearby markers when necessary; use campus markers while the dataset is small.

“Fit results,” zoom controls, reset view and pause motion are available.

Two campuses must not create hundreds of decorative or duplicate dots. Multi-campus companies count once globally and can appear under each supported affiliation.

University locations require sourced coordinates. Do not generate random coordinates or silently substitute unrelated geocodes.

Lazy-load the map/globe implementation. Reuse installed visualization dependencies when suitable. A 2D fallback and Table remain fully usable without WebGL. Never fetch/enrich every company merely because a user pans the globe.

8. Network view

The graph answers: “Which university communities produced these builders, projects and companies?” It does not imply who personally knows whom.

Node types: university, program/lab/community, person, project, venture. Shapes or icons and a legend distinguish them; color is secondary. Extend the existing entity model additively if projects are not yet modeled.

Allowed sourced relationships include:

Person → university: studied at / studies at / researched at, with dates when known.

Person → lab/community: member of / contributed to, with specific evidence.

Person → project: built / coauthored / contributed to, preserving the actual attribution.

Person → venture: founded, when evidenced.

Venture → program: participated in / won award at, with date and source.

Do not create person-to-person friendship edges because people attended the same university or event. Shared affiliation is a path through the institution node. Do not create a “warm introduction” feature without an explicitly supplied real relationship.

Default to a selected campus with programs grouped spatially. Expand a selected node by one hop; additional expansion is explicit. Show labels for selected and nearby nodes, not every node simultaneously. Limit the visible subgraph, for example to 150 nodes, with a truthful “Showing X of Y” indicator and search/filter controls.

Clicking an edge opens its relationship label, source and date. Clicking a person opens their work and any evidenced company links. Graph position, node degree and institution prestige never change the company score. Provide an accessible relationship list alternative.

