Research priority is an explicit research-work ordering, not an investment score. Use qualitative labels and a cited reason; allow user override. Keep the existing investment/research assessment formula and evidence-coverage gate unchanged unless explicitly instructed otherwise. Insufficient evidence displays “Unassessed,” never an invented numerical score.

Sort controls: research priority, supported assessment, latest substantive activity, recently discovered. “Latest activity” uses the event date; “Recently discovered” uses the discovery date. An old award imported today is not a new award.

Builder mode uses different columns: Person/project, attributed work, campus affiliation, latest artifact, venture link if evidenced, next research step. It must not turn every builder into an investable company.

10. Shared detail drawer

The drawer must answer the investment questions before displaying administrative metadata.

Header: company name, one-sentence product, website and public founder links; compact eligibility/research status.

Above the fold:

Why look: strongest supported reason to investigate.

Founder edge: concrete technical work, domain access or execution evidence.

Open question: the material uncertainty that could change the decision.

Primary action: Research company. Secondary actions: Save, Add to pipeline.

Below: Overview | Founders | Evidence | Activity | Notes.

Overview contains product/customer, differentiation, stage/funding, eligibility and assessment details. Clearly distinguish known facts, sourced founder claims and analyst judgment. Founders exposes actual artifacts and affiliation dates. Evidence shows short excerpts and direct source links with publication/retrieval dates. Notes remain user-editable and survive enrichment.

Distinguish negative evidence from absent evidence. Change “Strongest reason to pass: No stage evidence yet” to “Open question: Confirm stage and financing.” Do not translate “no funding events on record” into zero funding.

Where the user’s policy differs from verified public DRF criteria, label “Your scouting brief.” Do not imply official DRF endorsement of this tool, rubric or shortlist.

11. Shortlist, Signals, Pipeline and Sources

Shortlist

Use tabs: Ready to review | Needs research | Watching | Outside brief, mapping to existing queue IDs without destroying history. Keep manual saved state independent of qualification. A user saving a company does not make it eligible or assessed.

Ready to review records show the meeting rationale, key uncertainty and supporting evidence. Do not demand a fixed number of recommendations.

Empty state: “No companies ready for review yet.” Show the actual number needing research and a button to investigate them. If no candidates exist, offer source selection instead. Show suggested next research tasks rather than a large blank bordered box.

Signals

Compact chronological feed with university, company and event-type filters. Each item has company, substantive change, event date, source and short significance. Types include Product launch, Technical release, Award, Funding, Customer evidence and Team change.

Competition prizes should be labeled Award unless the source specifically calls them grants. Do not repeat the same methodological sentence below every item. Distinguish “Newly discovered” from “New event”; an initial scan is a baseline.

Pipeline

Suggested stages: New → Researching → Contact planned → Contacted → Meeting scheduled → Diligence → Passed. Watching can remain a saved queue. Migrate existing Meet/Meeting statuses only after inspecting their stored meaning; retain originals in history if ambiguous.

Enable add-to-pipeline directly from cards and drawers, then status changes and notes. A status change records the user's workflow; it does not send a message. Do not leave eight discovered candidates as inert chips beneath eight empty boxes.

Sources

Use a searchable compact table: Source, Campus, Family, Status, Last successful run, People, Projects, Ventures, Review yield, Action.

Status labels: Not configured / Ready to scan / Running / Complete / Partial / Blocked / Error. Display attempt and success timestamps separately. “Complete” means the configured run scope completed, not exhaustive ecosystem coverage. Keep full error details in a collapsible log.

Portfolio references have their own count and never inflate candidate totals. Replace implementation descriptions with concise useful status messages. Add Scan selected, Retry and Import public records where supported; clicking a source opens coverage and evidence details.

12. Research actions and cost control

Research company runs the existing bounded enrichment flow: identity → product → founder → stage/funding → evidence → assessment. Show actual progress, cancellation and partial results. Return useful findings incrementally; preserve the result if a later source fails.

Scan sources opens a small scope selector: Penn/Berkeley, source families and the existing page/enrichment budget. Show estimated cost only if calculable; otherwise show limits. No open-ended crawl, no background loop triggered by page navigation, no automatic outreach and no paid-service activation.

Cache by source/entity and use last successful results immediately. Reuse primary pages already fetched. Manual research should still work without optional API keys. Do not require an LLM for search, filtering, layout, deduplication keys or every render.

